  <items>
    <item><headword id="d0e32" type="heading" level="h1">Shapes and general appearance</headword></item>
    <item>
      <headword id="idp109742784">bachaill</headword>
      <description>clumsy, untidy, unkempt man – ’Se sàr bhachall [sic] a th’ann.</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <headword id="idp109744728">clabaisteir</headword>
      <description>large, clumsy person (male) rather than brawler as used by Dwelly.</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <headword id="idp109710384">blataillein</headword>
      <description>a very outsize unattractive [sic]. [NOTES: the following explanation and the next three words written in second hand.] Thàinig e ’s blatàillein de thè còmh’ ris. Also used to describe a crowd of people (prob. batallion), e.g. Thàinig blatàillein a steach.</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <headword id="idp109708368">pleabain</headword>
      <description>(used of a man) a skinny, miserable, useless looking character with no obvious personality.</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <headword id="idp472264">crabhàidseach</headword>
      <description>Seann chràbhaidseach – an old decrepit person (female). (Donald MacIntyre in Sporan Dhòmhnaill uses “cranàidseichean”.)</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <headword id="idp474496">sgraoid</headword>
      <description>(used of a woman) e.g. seann sgraoid. Untidy older woman who takes no care of her appearance. Used also as a derogatory term describing an unpopular person, probably female.</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <headword id="idp477520">pliobain</headword>
      <description>second in command, someone with no authority. [NOTES: note added above ‘n’ in ‘pliobain’ – (r).]</description>
